You can keep a return type annotation on your endpoint for editor and mypy support without FastAPI using it as the response model by setting `response_model=None`. This allows you to have any return type annotations you need without affecting your FastAPI application. Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Response

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/portal", response_model=None)
async def get_portal(teleport: bool = False) -> Response | dict:
    if teleport:
        return RedirectResponse(url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dQw4w9WgXcQ")
    return {"message": "Here's your interdimensional portal."}
```

This way, FastAPI will skip the response model generation while still allowing you to use type annotations for tooling support [2].

You can include custom headers in a `JSONResponse` by passing the headers as an additional parameter when returning the response. Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI
from fastapi.responses import JSONResponse

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/headers/")
def get_headers():
    content = {"message": "Hello World"}
    headers = {"X-Cat-Dog": "alone in the world", "Content-Language": "en-US"}
    return JSONResponse(content=content, headers=headers)
```

This code demonstrates how to create a `JSONResponse` with custom headers included in the response [1].

To turn your custom exception into a JSON error response in one place, you can create a custom exception handler for your exception class. You would define the handler using the `@app.exception_handler` decorator, similar to how it's done for `RequestValidationError` and `StarletteHTTPException`. 

Here is an example of how to implement this:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException
from fastapi.responses import JSONResponse

class MyCustomException(Exception):
    def __init__(self, name: str):
        self.name = name

app = FastAPI()

@app.exception_handler(MyCustomException)
async def my_custom_exception_handler(request, exc: MyCustomException):
    return JSONResponse(
        status_code=400,
        content={"detail": f"An error occurred: {exc.name}"},
    )

@app.get("/items/{item_id}")
async def read_item(item_id: int):
    if item_id == 3:
        raise MyCustomException(name="Item ID 3 is not allowed.")
    return {"item_id": item_id}
```

In this example, when `MyCustomException` is raised, the custom handler will return a JSON response with the error details instead of handling it in every endpoint individually. The excerpts do not cover how to define your own exception class or how to raise it.

You can use an `Enum` to restrict path parameter values in FastAPI by creating a subclass of `str` and `Enum`, defining the valid values as class attributes. Then, you can declare a path parameter in your path operation function using this enum class. Here's an example:

```python
from enum import Enum
from fastapi import FastAPI

class ModelName(str, Enum):
    alexnet = "alexnet"
    resnet = "resnet"
    lenet = "lenet"

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/models/{model_name}")
async def get_model(model_name: ModelName):
    if model_name is ModelName.alexnet:
        return {"model_name": model_name, "message": "Deep Learning FTW!"}
    if model_name.value == "lenet":
        return {"model_name": model_name, "message": "LeCNN all the images"}
    return {"model_name": model_name, "message": "Have some residuals"}
```

In this example, the `model_name` path parameter is restricted to the values defined in the `ModelName` enum [1][3][6].

You can revert to the old behavior of returning a `403 Forbidden` error by overriding the method `make_not_authenticated_error` in your security classes. You can create a subclass of `HTTPBearer` that returns a `403 Forbidden` error instead of the default `401 Unauthorized` error. Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import Depends, FastAPI, HTTPException, status
from fastapi.security import HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, HTTPBearer

app = FastAPI()

class HTTPBearer403(HTTPBearer):
    def make_not_authenticated_error(self) -> HTTPException:
        return HTTPException(
            status_code=status.HTTP_403_FORBIDDEN, detail="Not authenticated"
        )

CredentialsDep = Annotated[HTTPAuthorizationCredentials, Depends(HTTPBearer403())]

@app.get("/me")
def read_me(credentials: CredentialsDep):
    return {"message": "You are authenticated", "token": credentials.credentials}
```

This code snippet shows how to implement the old behavior by subclassing `HTTPBearer` [1].

You can achieve this by returning a `Response` or any subclass of it directly from your FastAPI endpoint. When you return a `Response`, FastAPI will pass it directly without doing any data conversion or validation with Pydantic models [9]. Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Response

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/custom-response/")
def custom_response():
    data = {"message": "This is a custom response"}
    return Response(content=data, media_type="application/json")
```

In this example, the data is returned exactly as specified without any conversion or validation by FastAPI.

To make a bytes field in your FastAPI response model come out as base64 in the JSON response, you can use the `ser_json_bytes` configuration in your Pydantic model. Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel

class DataOutput(BaseModel):
    description: str
    data: bytes

    model_config = {"ser_json_bytes": "base64"}

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/data")
def get_data() -> DataOutput:
    data = "hello".encode("utf-8")
    return DataOutput(description="A plumbus", data=data)
```

In this example, the `data` field will be serialized as base64 when generating the JSON response [2].

You can change the status code by using a `Response` parameter in your path operation function. This allows you to set the status code conditionally based on whether the item already exists or is being created. Here’s an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Response, status

app = FastAPI()

items = {"foo": {"name": "Fighters", "size": 6}}

@app.put("/items/{item_id}", status_code=200)
def upsert_item(item_id: str, name: str, size: int, response: Response):
    if item_id in items:
        item = items[item_id]
        item["name"] = name
        item["size"] = size
    else:
        item = {"name": name, "size": size}
        items[item_id] = item
        response.status_code = status.HTTP_201_CREATED
    return item
```

In this example, if the item does not exist, it is created and the status code is set to 201 "Created" using the `Response` parameter [3]. If the item exists, it returns the item with a status code of 200 "OK" by default [1].

To serve several apps with different domains over HTTPS from one server, you can use a **TLS Termination Proxy**. This proxy listens on the public IP address and port (typically port `443` for HTTPS) and handles the HTTPS requests. It can manage multiple HTTPS certificates using the **SNI (Server Name Indication)** extension, which allows it to serve multiple domains from a single IP address by selecting the appropriate certificate based on the requested domain [2][4]. The TLS Termination Proxy decrypts the requests and forwards them to the correct application running on the server [10]. 

Here’s a simplified example of how you might set it up:

```python
# Example of a TLS Termination Proxy configuration (not actual code)
# This is typically done in a configuration file for the proxy server
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name someapp.example.com;
    ssl_certificate /path/to/certificate1.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/to/key1.key;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8000;  # FastAPI app
    }
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name anotherapp.example.com;
    ssl_certificate /path/to/certificate2.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /path/to/key2.key;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:8001;  # Another FastAPI app
    }
}
```

This setup allows the TLS Termination Proxy to handle requests for multiple applications while only having one process listening on the public IP and port [1][4].

To serve the docs UI files yourself in FastAPI, you need to disable the automatic docs and mount a static files directory for the JavaScript and CSS files. Here’s how you can do it:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI
from fastapi.openapi.docs import (
    get_redoc_html,
    get_swagger_ui_html,
    get_swagger_ui_oauth2_redirect_html,
)
from fastapi.staticfiles import StaticFiles

app = FastAPI(docs_url=None, redoc_url=None)

app.mount("/static", StaticFiles(directory="static"), name="static")

@app.get("/docs", include_in_schema=False)
async def custom_swagger_ui_html():
    return get_swagger_ui_html(
        openapi_url=app.openapi_url,
        title=app.title + " - Swagger UI",
        oauth2_redirect_url=app.swagger_ui_oauth2_redirect_url,
        swagger_js_url="/static/swagger-ui-bundle.js",
        swagger_css_url="/static/swagger-ui.css",
    )

@app.get(app.swagger_ui_oauth2_redirect_url, include_in_schema=False)
async def swagger_ui_redirect():
    return get_swagger_ui_oauth2_redirect_html()

@app.get("/redoc", include_in_schema=False)
async def redoc_html():
    return get_redoc_html(
        openapi_url=app.openapi_url,
        title=app.title + " - ReDoc",
        redoc_js_url="/static/redoc.standalone.js",
    )
```

In this code, the automatic documentation is disabled by setting `docs_url` and `redoc_url` to `None`, and the static files are served from the `/static` directory [5][1]. You need to ensure that the JavaScript and CSS files are placed in the specified directory [2].

You can add a description and an external docs link to each tag group in your FastAPI docs by using the `openapi_tags` parameter when creating your `FastAPI` app instance. You need to provide a list of dictionaries, where each dictionary represents a tag and can include a `name`, `description`, and `externalDocs`. 

Here is an example:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

tags_metadata = [
    {
        "name": "users",
        "description": "Operations with users. The **login** logic is also here.",
    },
    {
        "name": "items",
        "description": "Manage items. So _fancy_ they have their own docs.",
        "externalDocs": {
            "description": "Items external docs",
            "url": "https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/",
        },
    },
]

app = FastAPI(openapi_tags=tags_metadata)
```

In this example, the `users` tag has a description, while the `items` tag has both a description and an external documentation link [1][2].

You can handle a request with YAML content type in FastAPI by using a Pydantic model to define the schema and manually parsing the YAML content. You can declare the request content type as YAML in the `openapi_extra` parameter and then use the `yaml` library to load the request body. Here's an example:

```python
import yaml
from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request
from pydantic import BaseModel, ValidationError

app = FastAPI()

class Item(BaseModel):
    name: str
    tags: list[str]

@app.post(
    "/items/",
    openapi_extra={
        "requestBody": {
            "content": {"application/x-yaml": {"schema": Item.model_json_schema()}},
            "required": True,
        },
    },
)
async def create_item(request: Request):
    raw_body = await request.body()
    try:
        data = yaml.safe_load(raw_body)
    except yaml.YAMLError:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="Invalid YAML")
    try:
        item = Item.model_validate(data)
    except ValidationError as e:
        raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail=e.errors(include_url=False))
    return item
```

In this code, the request body is read as bytes, parsed as YAML, and then validated using the Pydantic model [1][3].

To document the callback request that your `/invoices/` endpoint will send to the external developer's API, you need to create a callback `APIRouter` that contains the callback path operation(s). You can then use the `callbacks` parameter in your API's path operation decorator to pass the routes from that callback router [1]. 

Here is an example of how to write the callback documentation code:

```python
from fastapi import APIRouter, FastAPI
from pydantic import BaseModel, HttpUrl

app = FastAPI()

class InvoiceEvent(BaseModel):
    description: str
    paid: bool

class InvoiceEventReceived(BaseModel):
    ok: bool

invoices_callback_router = APIRouter()

@invoices_callback_router.post(
    "{$callback_url}/invoices/{$request.body.id}", response_model=InvoiceEventReceived
)
def invoice_notification(body: InvoiceEvent):
    pass

@app.post("/invoices/", callbacks=invoices_callback_router.routes)
def create_invoice(invoice: Invoice, callback_url: HttpUrl | None = None):
    return {"msg": "Invoice received"}
```

This code sets up the callback router and documents how the external API should handle the callback request [1][6]. The actual implementation of the callback request itself is not covered in the excerpts.
